findings.claim:
Several new hook reads go through LastResult.Store:
- store-marks counts every entry's mark;
- NodeFields' storeNotListed reads each row's entry;
- notListedChildren on the first expand;
- can-reveal's path.

T-010 run 3 then relies on Release finding the stopped store collected when the second scan starts. Any of these reads that keeps the store in a static field, a cached reflection target, a captured closure or a long-lived local keeps the store alive. Run 3 would then report released=false and fail for a reason in the test, not the app. The existing hook already learned this (SL-044 review M2): WeakStore is NoInlining and returns only a weak reference.

The design states no matching rule for the new reads. The invariant therefore holds only if each new read site remembers it. It cannot hold by construction, because nothing routes these reads through one helper.

Rule 2's pattern was written against the four guards in the file today and was not tried against a corpus of other spellings, as CC-007 asks. It is quiet on several ways a later figure property could guard by the store alone:
- `_session.Store == null ?`;
- `_session.Store is not { } ? … : …`;
- a get-accessor body (`{ get { return _session.Store is null ? …`);
- `_session?.Store is null ?`;
- a store-null test followed by an unrelated extra condition (`_session.Store is null || _isRoot ?`), which the rule's own text says it stays quiet on.

Its text also says 'Every figure text and ShareShown goes through HasFigures'. That is a claim about the whole class of figure properties, and the text never calls the rule a tripwire, as lesson #486 and Q-251 ask rule text to.

dimensions_checked.result:
